<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for the Developer ID build. Referenced from
  tauri.conf.json -> bundle.macOS.entitlements.

  Hardened runtime is REQUIRED for notarization, and it revokes capabilities by
  default rather than granting them. So the one key below buys back something
  the app actually does. Nothing should be added "just in case": the notary
  service does not care how long this list is, but every entry widens what a
  signed binary may do, and future readers have to justify each one.

  The failure mode this file guards is RELEASE-ONLY. An unsigned `tauri dev`
  build has no hardened runtime, so the microphone works there whether or not
  this file is correct — only the signed artifact goes silent. That is the same
  shape as the Android R8 bugs in acfcc64e4a / 7108fbf7b7: test voice capture on
  a NOTARIZED, quarantined build (downloaded through a browser), never on a
  locally built .app.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  Voice capture. Rust owns the whole audio lifecycle (voice/, MJX-96): cpal
	  opens a CoreAudio input stream IN THIS PROCESS, not in the webview, so the
	  webview's own getUserMedia grant is not what is being asked about here.
	  Without this key the hardened runtime denies the device and capture
	  returns SILENCE — no error, no TCC prompt, no log line.

	  NSMicrophoneUsageDescription in Info.plist is the other half. Both are
	  required; either one alone fails.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>

	<!--
	  DELIBERATELY ABSENT, with reasons — these are the ones people add by
	  reflex, and each would weaken the binary for nothing:

	  * com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit and
	    com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
	      Electron folklore. Electron needs them because V8 JITs inside the app
	      process. WKWebView does not: JavaScriptCore runs in Apple's own,
	      separately-signed com.apple.WebKit.WebContent XPC process, which
	      carries its own entitlements. Ours are irrelevant to it.

	  * com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
	      The only runtime dlopen in the tree is libloading -> gtk-layer-shell
	      (surface/layer_shell.rs), which is cfg(target_os = "linux"). Nothing
	      is loaded unsigned on macOS. Add this only if a signed build actually
	      fails library validation, and record what loaded.

	  * com.apple.security.app-sandbox and the network.* / files.* family
	      App Sandbox keys, and this is a non-sandboxed Developer ID build, so
	      they would do nothing. Sandboxing would also break portable-pty
	      spawning user shells, CGWindowListCopyWindowInfo in surface/mac.rs,
	      and keepawake's IOPMAssertion.

	  Screen Recording (the window-title read in surface/mac.rs) is a TCC grant,
	  not an entitlement — and that code preflights it and never requests it, so
	  there is no key and no usage-description string to add.
	-->
</dict>
</plist>
